---

<Layout title="Notes | Dew Point Calculator">
	<Breadcrumb crumbs={[
		{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
		{ label: 'Online tools', href: '/online-tools' },
		{ label: 'Dew Point Calculator' },
	]} />
	<h1>Dew Point Calculator</h1>
	<DewPointNav active="notes" />
	<h2>Quantities</h2>
	<p>
	The quantities in this calculator relate to the water content of air. The calculator uses the following definitions:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><b>Dew point (T<sub>d</sub>):</b> the temperature to which air must be cooled for water vapor to start to condense.</li>
		<li><b>Vapor pressure (e):</b> the partial pressure of water vapor in the air.</li>
		<li><b>Saturation vapor pressure (e<sub>s</sub>):</b> the highest vapor pressure that can exist in equilibrium with liquid water (or ice) at the air temperature.</li>
		<li><b>Relative humidity (RH):</b> vapor pressure as a percentage of saturation vapor pressure.</li>
		<li><b>Absolute humidity (ρ):</b> the mass of water vapor per unit volume of air.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>How they relate</h2>
	<p>
	Saturation vapor pressure depends only on temperature, and vapor pressure depends only on dew point: the vapor pressure of water in the air 
	equals the saturation vapor pressure at its dew point. Temperature and saturation vapor pressure therefore determine each other, as do dew point 
	and vapor pressure. Any two quantities other than one of these pairs determine all the others.
	</p>
	<p>
	Relative humidity is <span style="white-space: nowrap;">RH = e / e<sub>s</sub></span>, where e is vapor pressure and e<sub>s</sub> is saturation
	vapor pressure. Absolute humidity follows from the ideal gas law: <span style="white-space: nowrap;">ρ = e · M / (R · T)</span>, where M is the molar
	mass of water, R is the gas constant (8.31446 J/(mol·K)), and T is the absolute temperature.
	</p>
	<h2>Dew point and frost point</h2>
	<p>
	Below water's triple point (0.01°C / 32.018°F / 273.16 K), liquid water cannot exist in equilibrium with its vapor, so saturation is taken over ice
	using <a href="https://iapws.org/public/documents/MdUFK/MeltSub2011.pdf" target="_blank">the IAPWS formula for sublimation pressure</a>, and the dew
	point is called the frost point. The pressure at the triple point is 611.657 Pa (0.00604 atm / 0.0887 psi).
	</p>
	<h2>Limits and assumptions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Temperatures range from absolute zero to water's critical point (373.946°C / 705.103°F / 647.096 K), and vapor pressures from 0 to the
			critical pressure (22,064 kPa / 217.755 atm / 3,200.11 psi). Above the critical point, liquid and vapor are indistinguishable.</li>
		<li>Vapor pressures are for pure water vapor. In air at normal atmospheric pressure, the saturation vapor pressure is about 0.4% higher.</li>
		<li>Absolute humidity treats water vapor as an ideal gas. This is accurate to within about 0.1% at ordinary temperatures but becomes much less
			accurate near the critical point.</li>
		<li>Relative humidity above 100% (dew point > temperature) means supersaturated air, which can exist briefly but quickly
			condenses into cloud, fog, or frost.</li>
		<li>At absolute zero, saturation vapor pressure is 0, so relative and absolute humidity are undefined and shown as N/A.</li>
		<li>The calculator uses 18.015268 g/mol for water's molar mass (used for absolute humidity). This refers 
			to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna_Standard_Mean_Ocean_Water" target="_blank">Vienna Standard Mean Ocean Water (VSMOW)</a>, a 
			standardized isotopic composition of water representative of Earth's oceans.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Sources</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Source code for this calculator: <a href="https://github.com/crosscountrycoder/sanjayksrikanth" target="_blank">GitHub</a></li>
		<li><a href="https://iapws.org/public/documents/6dGkr/Supp-sat.pdf" target="_blank">Wagner-Pruss formula for saturation vapor pressure
			over liquid water</a>, accurate to within 72 parts per million (ppm) above the triple point</li>
		<li><a href="https://iapws.org/public/documents/MdUFK/MeltSub2011.pdf" target="_blank">IAPWS formula for sublimation pressure over ice</a>,
			accurate to within 100 ppm to -80°C</li>
		<li><a href="https://iapws.org/public/documents/3pv80/FundGuide26.pdf" target="_blank">IAPWS</a> for the molar mass of VSMOW</li>
	</ul>
</Layout>
